('how-to-choose-a-mineral-water-plant-in-gujar-khan',
'How to Choose a Mineral Water Plant in Gujar Khan',
'Not every water plant is the same. Here are the nine checks that separate a properly run bottled water operation from one you should walk away from.',
'<p>Search for a water plant near me in Gujar Khan and you will find a long list. Some of those operations run a full purification line with laboratory testing behind it. Others run a single filter and a filling tap. Both sell water in the same looking bottle at a similar price, and the customer usually cannot tell the difference until something goes wrong.</p>
<p>Here is what to actually check before you commit a household or an office to a supplier.</p>

<h2>1. Ask to see the food authority licence</h2>
<p>In Punjab, a bottled drinking water establishment must be approved by the <strong>Punjab Food Authority</strong>. This is not optional and it is not a formality. A legitimate plant will show you the licence without hesitation and will have the number available. If a supplier becomes evasive when you ask, that answer is itself the answer.</p>

<h2>2. Ask how many purification stages the plant actually runs</h2>
<p>The phrase mineral water gets used very loosely. A meaningful process includes sediment filtration, activated carbon, softening, micron filtration, reverse osmosis, re-mineralisation and a final disinfection step. Our own line runs <a href="/purification-process">eight stages</a>, and each one exists because it removes something the previous stage cannot.</p>
<p>Ask the supplier to name the stages. Anyone running the process can do it in ten seconds. Anyone who cannot is not running it.</p>

<h2>3. Ask whether the water is re-mineralised</h2>
<p>This is the question that separates the serious plants from the rest. Reverse osmosis strips almost everything from water, including calcium, magnesium, potassium and sodium. Water in that state is safe but flat, and it contributes nothing nutritionally.</p>
<p>A properly run plant adds minerals back under metered control after the membranes. That is what produces a consistent taste in January and in July. If a supplier does not re-mineralise, you are drinking demineralised water, whatever the label says.</p>

<h2>4. Check for a batch code and filling date on the bottle</h2>
<p>Every bottle should carry a batch number and filling date. Without them, nothing is traceable. With them, a single photograph lets the plant pull the exact production record for the day your bottle was filled, including laboratory results.</p>
<p>Batch coding costs a plant almost nothing. Its absence tells you the plant is not set up to be held accountable.</p>

<h2>5. Ask what happens to returned 19 litre bottles</h2>
<p>This matters more than most customers realise. A returned bottle should be inspected under light, sniff checked, pre-rinsed, washed internally with food grade caustic solution, rinsed with treated water and sanitised before refilling. Bottles that are scratched, stained, deformed or carrying any odour should be permanently retired.</p>
<p>Some operations simply rinse and refill. A bottle that once held kerosene or paint thinner cannot be recovered by rinsing, and no amount of purification upstream fixes a contaminated container.</p>

<h2>6. Ask about the security deposit terms in writing</h2>
<p>Most suppliers take a deposit on a 19 litre bottle. Ours is Rs 1,500 and it is refundable. What varies wildly between suppliers is what happens when you want it back.</p>
<p>Ask three questions: what is the deduction schedule, who inspects the bottle and where, and how long does the refund take. A supplier who has published answers has thought about it. A supplier who improvises the answer will improvise the deduction too. Our full <a href="/damage-policy">damage policy</a> and <a href="/refund-policy">refund policy</a> are on this website precisely so nobody has to ask.</p>

<h2>7. Test the delivery promise before you rely on it</h2>
<p>Reliability in this business is measured in summer, not in winter. Anyone can deliver in March. The question is whether the vehicle still arrives in the third week of June when demand doubles and half the fleet is overloaded.</p>
<p>Ask existing customers in your area. In a town like Gujar Khan, reputation travels faster than any advertisement.</p>

<h2>8. Look at the total cost, not the headline price</h2>
<p>Compare like with like. A refill at Rs 250 with free delivery is not the same as a refill at Rs 230 with a Rs 50 delivery charge. Check whether delivery is genuinely free, whether there is a minimum order, and whether the deposit is refundable in full.</p>
<p>For high volume users, also ask about the per litre rate at the plant. Ours is <a href="/bulk-water-calculator">Rs 6 per litre</a> for facility filling, which changes the economics entirely for caterers and canteens.</p>

<h2>9. Ask to see the plant</h2>
<p>This is the simplest test of all. A plant that is properly run is happy to show you the filling room, the bottle washing station and the laboratory. A plant that is not will find a reason why today is not convenient.</p>

<h2>The short version</h2>
<p>Licence, stages, re-mineralisation, batch coding, bottle washing, written deposit terms, summer reliability, true total cost, and a willingness to show you the plant. Nine checks, ten minutes, and it will save you a great deal of trouble later.</p>
<p>If you would like to put Pak-Everests through all nine, <a href="/contact">contact us</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp. We will answer every one of them.</p>',
'Buying Guide','water plant near me, mineral water plant, Gujar Khan, buying guide','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'How to Choose a Mineral Water Plant in Gujar Khan | 9 Checks',
'Nine checks before choosing a bottled water supplier in Gujar Khan: licence, purification stages, re-mineralisation, batch coding and deposit terms.',
'water plant in Gujar Khan, best water plant in Gujar Khan, mineral water plant, water plant near me',
1,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 45 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 45 DAY)),

('why-mineral-water-is-better-than-plain-ro-water',
'Why Mineral Water Is Better Than Plain RO Water',
'Reverse osmosis makes water pure. It also makes it empty. Here is why what happens after the membrane matters as much as the membrane itself.',
'<p>Reverse osmosis is a remarkable technology. It pushes water through a membrane whose pores are small enough to reject dissolved salts, heavy metals, nitrates, bacteria and viruses. If you want water free of contamination, RO is how you get there.</p>
<p>But there is a second half to the story that most suppliers skip, and it is the half that determines whether the water in your glass is genuinely good to drink.</p>

<h2>What RO actually removes</h2>
<p>The membrane does not distinguish between harmful and useful. It rejects lead, arsenic and nitrates, which is exactly what you want. It also rejects calcium, magnesium, potassium and bicarbonate, which you do not.</p>
<p>Water leaving an RO membrane typically carries a total dissolved solids reading of under 20 mg per litre. That is close to laboratory grade purity. It is also, by every tasting panel ever run, flat and unsatisfying.</p>

<h2>The taste problem is a mineral problem</h2>
<p>If you have ever drunk water that felt thin, or that somehow did not quench thirst properly, you have almost certainly drunk unmineralised RO water. Minerals are what give water body on the palate. Bicarbonate in particular gives it a soft, slightly sweet character.</p>
<p>Tasting studies consistently place the preferred TDS band between roughly 100 and 300 mg per litre. Below that, water tastes empty. Above about 500, it starts to taste heavy. <a href="/product/19-litre-refill-bottle">Pak-Everests targets 120 to 200 mg per litre</a>, which sits in the middle of the preferred band.</p>

<h2>The nutritional argument</h2>
<p>Nobody meets their daily mineral requirement from water alone, and no honest supplier claims otherwise. But water is a genuinely useful contributor for one specific reason: minerals dissolved in water are in ionic form, which the body absorbs efficiently without the digestive competition that comes with some food sources.</p>
<h3>Calcium</h3>
<p>Roughly 99 percent of body calcium sits in bone and teeth. The rest runs muscle contraction, nerve signalling and blood clotting. For growing children and for older adults losing bone density, a steady daily contribution matters.</p>
<h3>Magnesium</h3>
<p>A cofactor in over three hundred enzyme reactions. Where calcium tells a muscle to contract, magnesium tells it to relax. Night cramps and poor sleep quality are both associated with low magnesium status.</p>
<h3>Potassium</h3>
<p>The counterweight to sodium. It promotes sodium excretion through the kidneys and supports the electrical gradient the heart depends on for rhythm.</p>
<h3>Sodium</h3>
<p>Genuinely necessary, and genuinely over consumed from processed food rather than from water. In a Potohar summer, sodium lost through sweat has to be replaced. We keep it deliberately low at 8 to 20 mg per litre, far below the 200 mg per litre ceiling.</p>
<p>Our full <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">mineral profile page</a> sets out the target range for each one alongside the WHO and PSQCA limits.</p>

<h2>The pH question</h2>
<p>Unmineralised RO water tends to sit slightly on the acidic side of neutral, because it has no buffering capacity. Add bicarbonate alkalinity and the pH settles into a stable, slightly alkaline 7.2 to 7.8, which is where water tastes cleanest and sits most comfortably on the stomach.</p>

<h2>Why controlled addition beats natural hardness</h2>
<p>Here is the argument for doing it this way rather than simply filtering groundwater lightly and calling it mineral water.</p>
<p>Groundwater mineral content varies with the season, the water table and the specific aquifer. Filter it lightly and the mineral content of your bottle changes month to month, along with whatever else the aquifer happens to be carrying. Strip it to purity and rebuild it under metered control, and every bottle is identical, all year, by design.</p>
<p>Purity first, minerals second. That sequence is what makes consistency possible.</p>

<h2>How to tell if your water is re-mineralised</h2>
<p>Ask the supplier for the TDS figure. If it is under 50 mg per litre, the water is not re-mineralised. If the supplier does not know the figure, that tells you something too. A plant that controls the process knows its numbers.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>RO alone gets you safe water. RO followed by controlled re-mineralisation gets you water that is safe, consistent, contributes something useful, and that people actually want to drink. The membrane is the beginning of the process, not the end of it.</p>',
'Water & Health','mineral water, RO water, TDS, calcium, magnesium','Pak-Everests Team',6,
'Why Mineral Water Is Better Than Plain RO Water | Pak-Everests',
'Reverse osmosis removes contaminants and minerals alike. Why controlled re-mineralisation matters for taste, pH and the minerals in your water.',
'mineral water vs RO water, re-mineralisation, TDS in drinking water, calcium magnesium water',
1,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 32 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 32 DAY)),

('how-much-water-should-you-drink-in-potohar-summer',
'How Much Water Should You Drink in a Potohar Summer',
'A practical hydration guide for Gujar Khan, Rawalpindi and Islamabad, built around what the heat here actually does to the body.',
'<p>The standard advice is eight glasses a day. It is a reasonable starting point and it is also almost useless in a Potohar June, when the afternoon sits above forty degrees and anyone working outdoors loses more than that through sweat before lunch.</p>
<p>Here is a more practical way to think about it.</p>

<h2>Start with body weight, then adjust</h2>
<p>A workable baseline is <strong>30 to 35 millilitres per kilogram of body weight per day</strong> in normal conditions. For a 70 kilogram adult that is roughly 2.1 to 2.5 litres, which is where the eight glasses figure comes from.</p>
<p>Then adjust upward for the things that actually drive loss:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heat above 35 degrees:</strong> add 500 ml to 1 litre</li>
<li><strong>Outdoor or physical work:</strong> add 500 ml to 1.5 litres depending on intensity</li>
<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> add roughly 500 ml per hour of activity</li>
<li><strong>Fever:</strong> add roughly 200 ml for each degree above normal</li>
<li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> add roughly 300 ml. While breastfeeding, add roughly 700 ml</li>
</ul>
<p>A construction worker on Adiala Road in June can genuinely need five to six litres across the day. That is not exaggeration, it is arithmetic.</p>

<h2>Electrolytes matter as much as volume</h2>
<p>Sweat is not just water. It carries sodium, potassium, magnesium and chloride. Replace heavy sweat loss with pure water alone and you dilute what remains, which is why people who drink plenty of water in the heat still get cramps and headaches.</p>
<p>This is one of the practical arguments for mineral water over demineralised water in a hot climate. Our <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">mineral profile</a> carries sodium, potassium, calcium and magnesium at levels that support electrolyte balance through the day. For genuinely heavy sweat loss, add an oral rehydration solution as well.</p>

<h2>Read the signals your body gives</h2>
<h3>Urine colour is the simplest test</h3>
<p>Pale straw colour means adequately hydrated. Dark yellow means drink now. Amber means you are already meaningfully dehydrated.</p>
<h3>Thirst arrives late</h3>
<p>By the time you feel thirsty you have usually already lost around two percent of body water. In the heat, drink to schedule rather than to thirst.</p>
<h3>Early warning signs</h3>
<p>Headache, difficulty concentrating, unexplained fatigue and irritability all show up before serious dehydration. In children and older adults, confusion and unusual drowsiness are warning signs that need immediate attention.</p>

<h2>A practical daily schedule</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>On waking:</strong> one full glass. You have gone eight hours without water.</li>
<li><strong>With breakfast:</strong> one glass.</li>
<li><strong>Mid morning:</strong> one to two glasses, more if you are outdoors.</li>
<li><strong>Before lunch:</strong> one glass, about thirty minutes before eating.</li>
<li><strong>Afternoon, the critical window:</strong> two to three glasses between 2 and 5 PM, which is when heat loss peaks here.</li>
<li><strong>Evening:</strong> one to two glasses.</li>
<li><strong>Before sleep:</strong> half a glass. Enough to help, not enough to wake you.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Specific groups</h2>
<h3>School children</h3>
<p>Children dehydrate faster than adults because of their surface area to volume ratio, and they are poor at noticing it. Send a sealed <a href="/product/500ml-pack-of-12-mix">500 ml bottle</a> with the lunch box and ask the school to allow drinking during class in summer.</p>
<h3>Office workers</h3>
<p>Air conditioning is drying, and the sensation of thirst is weaker in a cool room. Keep a bottle on the desk where you can see it. Visibility does more for intake than intention.</p>
<h3>Older adults</h3>
<p>The thirst mechanism weakens with age, which is why dehydration in elderly people is common and often missed. Scheduled drinking matters more than waiting for thirst.</p>
<h3>Ramadan</h3>
<p>Concentrate intake between iftar and sehri, spread across the evening rather than taken in one or two large volumes, which the body simply excretes. Reduce salty and fried food, which increases fluid demand through the following day.</p>

<h2>Can you drink too much?</h2>
<p>Yes, although it is rare. Drinking several litres in a very short period can dilute blood sodium dangerously. Spread intake through the day rather than compensating in one sitting, and anyone with a kidney or heart condition should follow the fluid limits set by their physician rather than any general guidance.</p>

<h2>Keep good water within reach</h2>
<p>The single biggest determinant of how much water people drink is how easy it is to reach. A dispenser in the room, a bottle on the desk, a pack in the car. Everything else is secondary.</p>
<p>Pak-Everests delivers free across Gujar Khan, Rawalpindi and Islamabad. <a href="/order">Place an order</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp.</p>
<p><em>This article is general information and is not medical advice. Anyone with a kidney condition, heart condition or fluid restriction should follow the guidance of their physician.</em></p>',
'Water & Health','hydration, summer, health, Potohar','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'How Much Water Should You Drink in a Potohar Summer | Pak-Everests',
'How much water to drink in a Potohar summer, by body weight, heat and activity, plus the warning signs and a practical daily schedule.',
'how much water to drink, hydration guide Pakistan, summer hydration, drinking water health',
0,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 24 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 24 DAY)),

('custom-label-water-bottles-for-weddings-and-corporate-events',
'Custom Label Water Bottles for Weddings and Corporate Events',
'What custom printed bottles cost, how long they take, what artwork you need, and the mistakes that ruin an otherwise good design.',
'<p>A branded water bottle is the only item at an event that every single guest picks up, holds for several minutes and reads. Not the banner, not the menu card, not the stage backdrop. The bottle.</p>
<p>That is why custom label printing has become one of our most requested services, and it is also why the details are worth getting right.</p>

<h2>What you can print on</h2>
<p>We print custom labels on <strong>500 ml</strong> and <strong>1.5 litre</strong> bottles today, and the <a href="/product/350ml-pack-of-24">350 ml bottle</a> joins the range at launch.</p>
<p>The 500 ml is by far the most popular for events. It is the size a guest finishes, it sits neatly on a table setting, and it holds a full label area at eye level. The 1.5 litre suits boardroom tables and head tables where a shared bottle is more appropriate.</p>

<h2>What people use it for</h2>
<h3>Weddings and family events</h3>
<p>Couple names and the wedding date, mehndi and barat themed designs, aqiqah and walima bottles. Guests genuinely take these home, which is the point.</p>
<h3>Corporate offices</h3>
<p>Reception areas, meeting rooms and client visits. A branded bottle in front of a client says something about attention to detail before anyone speaks.</p>
<h3>Conferences, seminars and exhibitions</h3>
<p>Event branding, sponsor logos, dates and venue. Exhibition stands use them as the give away that costs least and gets used most.</p>
<h3>Hotels, restaurants and clinics</h3>
<p>House branded table water at a fraction of the cost of an imported brand.</p>
<h3>Political and welfare campaigns</h3>
<p>Campaign branding and welfare organisation identity on relief distribution.</p>

<h2>Artwork requirements</h2>
<p>This is where most delays happen, so it is worth reading carefully.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Format:</strong> AI, PSD, PDF, EPS or a high resolution PNG</li>
<li><strong>Resolution:</strong> 300 DPI at final print size. A logo pulled from a website at 72 DPI will print soft and there is no way around that</li>
<li><strong>Colour mode:</strong> CMYK. Convert before sending, because RGB colours shift on press</li>
<li><strong>Bleed:</strong> 3 mm on every side</li>
<li><strong>Text safety:</strong> keep important text at least 5 mm inside the trim, because the label wraps around a curve</li>
<li><strong>Fonts:</strong> outlined or supplied with the file</li>
</ul>
<p>No artwork? Our design team will create it. Send your logo, your brand colours, the text you want and a reference you like, and we produce concepts for you. Use the <a href="/custom-label-request">label customisation form</a> and attach whatever you have.</p>

<h2>What must stay on the label</h2>
<p>Every bottle sold in Punjab must carry the statutory information required by the Punjab Food Authority: the plant name and licence details, batch code, filling date and volume. This occupies a defined strip on the label and cannot be removed or covered by artwork. Our design team handles the layout so it never interferes with your branding.</p>

<h2>Timeline</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Typical time</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Brief received and quotation issued</td><td>Same day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Design concepts, where we create the artwork</td><td>2 to 3 working days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Revisions and proof approval</td><td>1 to 2 working days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Label printing</td><td>3 to 5 working days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bottle filling, labelling and delivery</td><td>2 to 3 working days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Plan for <strong>seven to twelve working days</strong> end to end. For a wedding, start three weeks out. Rush production is sometimes possible at a surcharge, but proof approval is the stage that cannot be compressed, because printing the wrong thing quickly helps nobody.</p>

<h2>The five mistakes that ruin a good design</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Low resolution logo.</strong> The most common problem by a distance. Get the original file from whoever designed it.</li>
<li><strong>Text too close to the edge.</strong> The label wraps around a curved bottle. Text at the margin disappears around the back.</li>
<li><strong>Very fine text.</strong> Anything under 6 point is unreadable on a curved surface at arm length.</li>
<li><strong>Dark heavy backgrounds.</strong> They look striking on screen and show every handling mark and condensation drop in reality.</li>
<li><strong>Approving the proof without reading it.</strong> Check the spelling of every name and the date. Twice. Once printed, it is printed.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Pricing</h2>
<p>Custom label pricing depends on bottle size, quantity, the number of print colours and whether we create the artwork. Quantity moves the price most, because label printing has a fixed setup cost that spreads across the run. Send your requirement through the <a href="/custom-label-request">customisation form</a> and we will issue a written quotation the same day.</p>

<h2>Getting started</h2>
<p>Tell us the bottle size, the quantity, the occasion and the date you need it. Attach your logo and any reference design. Everything else we handle.</p>
<p><a href="/custom-label-request">Submit your label brief</a> or WhatsApp 0333 5592206.</p>',
'Custom Labels','custom label, branding, weddings, corporate events','Pak-Everests Team',6,
'Custom Label Water Bottles for Weddings and Events | Pak-Everests',
'Custom printed water bottle labels for weddings, offices and events: artwork requirements, timeline, pricing and the five mistakes to avoid.',
'custom label water bottles, personalised water bottles Pakistan, wedding water bottles, branded water bottles',
1,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 16 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 16 DAY)),

('19-litre-water-bottle-price-and-deposit-explained',
'The 19 Litre Water Bottle: Price, Deposit and How the System Works',
'A plain explanation of what you pay, what you get back, and why the deposit exists at all.',
'<p>The 19 litre bottle is the single most common way water is bought in Pakistan, and it is also the arrangement customers most often find confusing. Two numbers get quoted, one is refundable and one is not, and suppliers are not always clear about which is which.</p>
<p>Here is exactly how it works at Pak-Everests.</p>

<h2>The two numbers</h2>
<h3>Rs 250 for the water</h3>
<p>This is the price of a refill. You pay it every time a full bottle arrives. It includes free delivery to your door anywhere in our <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a>, with no minimum order.</p>
<h3>Rs 1,500 as a refundable deposit for the bottle</h3>
<p>You pay this <strong>once per bottle</strong>, on your first delivery. It is not a payment for water and it is never adjusted against a refill. It secures the bottle itself, which remains our property throughout. When you stop the service and return the bottle in a usable condition, you get the full Rs 1,500 back.</p>

<h2>What your first delivery costs</h2>
<p>If you take one bottle: Rs 1,500 deposit plus Rs 250 for the water, so <strong>Rs 1,750</strong> on day one.</p>
<p>If you take two bottles, which most offices do so there is always a spare: Rs 3,000 deposit plus Rs 500, so <strong>Rs 3,500</strong>.</p>
<p>Every delivery after that is simply Rs 250 per bottle. Hand over the empty, take the full one, pay Rs 250.</p>

<h2>Why a deposit exists at all</h2>
<p>A food grade 19 litre polycarbonate bottle is an expensive item, and it is designed to be filled and returned many times over several years. Without a deposit, bottles disappear. They get used to store diesel, sold for scrap, or simply never come back. Every bottle lost has to be replaced out of the price of water, which means customers who return their bottles subsidise those who do not.</p>
<p>The deposit removes that. It is fully refundable, so it costs an honest customer nothing.</p>

<h2>Getting the deposit back</h2>
<ol>
<li>Tell us on WhatsApp that you want to close the account and how many bottles you are returning.</li>
<li>Hand the empties to the delivery team on the next route, or bring them to the plant.</li>
<li>The team inspects each bottle at the door in your presence and issues a collection receipt.</li>
<li>Cash is settled at the door, or a transfer is completed within seven working days.</li>
</ol>
<p>The full process, including timelines, is in our <a href="/refund-policy">refund policy</a>.</p>

<h2>What can be deducted</h2>
<p>Scratches, scuffs, faded printing and general marks from years of use are <strong>never</strong> charged. Those are expected.</p>
<p>Deductions apply only where a bottle is lost, cracked, punctured, deformed by heat, or contaminated by chemicals, fuel or paint. The full schedule with the exact figure for each case is published in our <a href="/damage-policy">damage policy</a>, and every assessment is made at your door with a photograph, not later behind a closed gate.</p>
<p>The one we are strict about is contamination. A bottle that has held kerosene or paint thinner cannot be returned to a food grade fleet by any washing process. It is destroyed.</p>

<h2>How long a bottle lasts you</h2>
<p>19 litres is roughly 95 glasses.</p>
<ul>
<li>A family of four to five: two to three days</li>
<li>A small office of ten: about one working day</li>
<li>A single person or a couple: four to six days</li>
</ul>
<p>If a bottle is lasting your household more than a week, consider the <a href="/product/12-litre-water-bottle">12 litre bottle</a> instead so the water is always fresh.</p>

<h2>Keep your first receipt</h2>
<p>It records your deposit. If you lose it, we refund against the bottle count recorded against your name, address and phone number in our system, which we maintain for every deposit customer. But keeping the receipt makes it simpler for everyone.</p>

<h2>Storage that protects the water and the bottle</h2>
<ul>
<li>Out of direct sunlight, and away from heaters and cooking areas</li>
<li>Never near detergent, paint, fuel or pesticide, because plastic transmits odour</li>
<li>Not stacked more than two bottles high</li>
<li>Wipe the neck and cap before mounting on a dispenser</li>
<li>Use within three to four days of opening</li>
</ul>

<h2>Start the service</h2>
<p><a href="/order">Place an order online</a>, or WhatsApp 0333 5592206 with your name, complete address and the number of bottles. Same day delivery on orders placed before 4:00 PM.</p>',
'Products','19 litre bottle, security deposit, pricing','Pak-Everests Team',5,
'19 Litre Water Bottle Price and Rs 1,500 Deposit Explained | Pak-Everests',
'What you pay for a 19 litre water bottle, why the Rs 1,500 security deposit exists, how to get it back in full, and what can be deducted.',
'19 liters water bottle, water bottle deposit, 19 litre bottle price Pakistan, water refill price',
0,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 9 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 9 DAY)),

('starting-a-water-distribution-business-in-potohar',
'Starting a Water Distribution Business in the Potohar Region',
'What an area distributorship actually involves: the investment, the daily work, the margins and the mistakes that sink new distributors.',
'<p>Water distribution looks simple from outside. Buy at one price, sell at another, deliver in between. In practice the businesses that survive are the ones that got a small number of unglamorous things right from the start.</p>
<p>If you are considering an area distributorship, here is an honest description of what it involves.</p>

<h2>Why the demand is real here</h2>
<p>Groundwater quality across much of the Potohar belt is variable, and awareness of it has risen sharply. Housing schemes along Adiala Road, Bahria Town and the Rawat corridor keep expanding. Offices, schools, clinics and marts all now treat bottled water as a standing requirement rather than an occasional purchase.</p>
<p>This is a repeat purchase category with genuine customer stickiness. A household that is happy with its supplier rarely changes. That cuts both ways: it is hard to win an account and hard to lose one.</p>

<h2>What you need to start</h2>
<h3>A delivery vehicle</h3>
<p>A Suzuki pickup or a loader rickshaw is the standard starting point. Capacity matters more than speed. Route density is what makes the economics work, and a vehicle that carries forty bottles instead of twenty halves your fuel cost per delivery.</p>
<h3>Covered storage</h3>
<p>A clean, dry, covered space away from direct sunlight, heat, and any chemical, fuel or paint. This is a food product and storage discipline is not optional. Storage also has to be secure, because bottle stock is the second largest asset in the business after the vehicle.</p>
<h3>Working capital</h3>
<p>Opening stock, the security deposit under the distributor agreement, fuel, and enough cash to carry credit customers for a month. Underestimating the last of these is the most common reason new distributors run into trouble.</p>
<h3>A rider or two</h3>
<p>Delivery staff are the face of the business. Customers judge the brand entirely by the person at the door.</p>

<h2>The daily work, honestly described</h2>
<p>Morning route loading. Deliveries from roughly 8 AM. Bottle collection alongside every delivery. Cash settlement at each door. Afternoon route for the second half of the territory. Evening reconciliation of cash, stock and empty bottles. New account calls fitted around the route.</p>
<p>The hardest part is not the delivering. It is the reconciliation, every single day, without exception. Distributors who let bottle counts and cash drift for a week are the ones who discover a hole they cannot explain.</p>

<h2>Where the money is made</h2>
<h3>Route density</h3>
<p>Twenty deliveries within two kilometres beats twenty deliveries spread across fifteen. Build a territory outward from a dense core rather than chasing scattered accounts across the map.</p>
<h3>Institutional accounts</h3>
<p>One school, hospital, factory or office block can equal thirty households in volume, on a single stop, with a single payment. These accounts take longer to win and are worth the effort.</p>
<h3>Standing orders</h3>
<p>A fixed weekly schedule lets you plan the route, the stock and the vehicle. One off orders are the least profitable work you will do.</p>
<h3>Bottle discipline</h3>
<p>Every uncontrolled bottle is dead capital. Distributors who track bottles by customer keep their working capital working.</p>

<h2>The four mistakes that sink new distributors</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Uncontrolled credit.</strong> Extending credit to win accounts, then finding a month of sales sitting in other people pockets. Set a limit per customer and hold it.</li>
<li><strong>Poor bottle tracking.</strong> Bottles issued and never recorded. By month six nobody knows where a hundred bottles went.</li>
<li><strong>Over promising delivery times.</strong> Promising a slot you cannot hold in summer loses accounts permanently. Under promise.</li>
<li><strong>Neglecting storage hygiene.</strong> One contaminated batch, one complaint to the food authority, and the territory reputation takes a year to rebuild.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What Pak-Everests provides</h2>
<ul>
<li>A defined and protected territory with exclusivity, subject to targets</li>
<li>Confidential distributor pricing with volume incentives</li>
<li>Reliable supply against confirmed orders, with priority over one off customers</li>
<li>A named account manager rather than a call centre</li>
<li>Point of sale material, banners and branding support</li>
<li>Product and hygiene training for your delivery team</li>
<li>Prompt free replacement of any product defective at our end</li>
</ul>
<p>Full commercial terms including territory, security deposit, payment terms and targets are published openly on our <a href="/distributor-terms">distributor terms page</a>. We publish them because a distributor who understands the terms before signing is a distributor who stays.</p>

<h2>How to apply</h2>
<p>Complete the <a href="/distributor-application">distributor application form</a> with your area, storage capacity, vehicle details and target volume. We review every application, meet shortlisted applicants at the plant, and issue a formal agreement setting out territory, pricing, security and targets.</p>
<p>Territories currently open across Gujar Khan, Mandra, Daultala, Bewal, Kallar Syedan, Rawat, Adiala Road and the wider Rawalpindi and Islamabad market.</p>
<p>Questions first? WhatsApp 0332 2901309.</p>',
'Business','distribution, business, franchise, Potohar','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'Starting a Water Distribution Business in Potohar | Pak-Everests',
'What a water distributorship really involves: vehicle and storage needs, working capital, the daily routine, and where the margin actually comes from.',
'water distribution business, become a water distributor, distributorship Pakistan, water business Rawalpindi',
0,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 4 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 4 DAY));


