('is-reverse-osmosis-water-safe-to-drink-every-day',
'Is Reverse Osmosis Water Safe to Drink Every Day?',
'Reverse osmosis makes water extremely pure, but pure is not the same as healthy. Here is the honest answer, and why re-mineralisation is the part that matters.',
'<p>Yes, reverse osmosis water is safe to drink every day, as long as the minerals that the membrane removes are added back afterwards. That single sentence is the whole answer, and the rest of this article explains why it is true and what to look for when you buy water in Gujar Khan, Rawalpindi or Islamabad.</p>
<p>Reverse osmosis, usually shortened to RO, is one of the most powerful filtration methods available. At Pak-Everests it sits in the middle of our <a href="/purification-process">eight stage purification process</a>, and it does a job that no simple filter can match. But RO on its own is only half of a good process. The other half is putting the good minerals back.</p>

<h2>What does reverse osmosis actually remove?</h2>
<p>RO pushes water through a membrane with pores so small that almost nothing except a water molecule can pass through. On the way, it removes dissolved salts, heavy metals, nitrates, most bacteria and viruses, and the general load of dissolved solids that raw groundwater in the Potohar region often carries.</p>
<p>This is exactly what you want. Groundwater around Gujar Khan and much of Rawalpindi can be hard, high in dissolved solids, and in some areas carries traces of things you do not want to drink for years on end. RO strips all of that out and gives you a clean base to work from.</p>

<h2>So why is pure RO water not the finished product?</h2>
<p>Here is the part most people are never told. The RO membrane cannot tell the difference between a harmful salt and a helpful mineral. When it removes the bad dissolved solids, it also removes calcium, magnesium, potassium and a little sodium, the same minerals your body uses every day.</p>
<p>Water that comes straight off an RO membrane is therefore extremely clean but also flat and empty. It is safe in the sense that it will not make you ill, but it tastes dull, and over the long term you are drinking water that gives you nothing back. That is why serious plants do not stop at RO.</p>

<h3>The re-mineralisation step</h3>
<p>After the membranes, Pak-Everests adds calcium, magnesium, potassium and sodium back into the water under metered control. You can read the detail on our <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">minerals and benefits page</a>, but the short version is this. Re-mineralisation is what turns clean water into good water. It restores the fresh, rounded taste, and it means the water contributes to your daily mineral intake instead of nothing.</p>
<p>If a supplier runs RO but does not re-mineralise, you are drinking demineralised water no matter what the label says. Ask the question directly. A properly run plant will answer it in one sentence.</p>

<h2>Does RO water remove minerals from your body?</h2>
<p>This is a common worry and the answer is reassuring. Water is not a significant route by which the body loses minerals. You get most of your minerals from food. However, water that is balanced and re-mineralised is simply better than water that is empty, both for taste and for the small but real contribution it makes. The goal is balance, not extremes, and balanced mineral water is what a good plant is set up to deliver every single day.</p>

<h2>How do you know the water is genuinely safe?</h2>
<p>Safety is not a feeling, it is a process backed by records. When you buy from a <strong>Punjab Food Authority</strong> approved plant like Pak-Everests, the water has passed through a controlled line and every batch is traceable. Look for three things on any bottle you buy every day.</p>
<ul>
<li>A visible batch code and filling date, so the exact production run can be traced.</li>
<li>A tamper evident seal that shows instantly if the bottle has been opened.</li>
<li>A supplier who will let you see the plant and name the purification stages without hesitation.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you would like to see how we choose a supplier the careful way, our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-a-mineral-water-plant-in-gujar-khan">how to choose a mineral water plant in Gujar Khan</a> walks through nine checks in ten minutes.</p>

<h2>Which Pak-Everests pack is best for daily drinking?</h2>
<p>For homes and offices that drink water every day, the <a href="/product/19-litre-refill-bottle">19 litre refill bottle</a> is the most economical choice, at Rs 250 per refill with free delivery across our <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a>. For desks, meetings and travel, the <a href="/product/500ml-pack-of-12-pure">500 ml packs</a> and <a href="/products">1.5 litre packs</a> keep the same re-mineralised water in a portable form. Whichever size you choose, the water inside is identical.</p>

<h2>Is RO water fine for children and older family members?</h2>
<p>Yes, re-mineralised RO water suits everyone in the household, including children and older relatives, because it is both clean and balanced. Children are more affected by waterborne germs than adults, so the disinfection steps in a proper process matter for them in particular. Older family members often benefit from water that is easy to drink and consistent in taste, which quietly encourages them to drink enough through a hot Potohar day. The point, once again, is that the water must be re-mineralised rather than left empty. Balanced mineral water is right for every age in the home, from a toddler to a grandparent.</p>

<h2>How does a plant keep RO water consistent batch after batch?</h2>
<p>Consistency is the quiet mark of a serious plant. Because reverse osmosis first strips the water back to the same clean base every time, the re-mineralisation blend that follows can be dosed to the same balance on every run. That is how the water tastes the same in a January delivery and a July one, even though the raw groundwater around Gujar Khan shifts with the seasons and the wells. Every batch also carries a code and a filling date, so a single bottle can be traced back to the exact run it came from. If a laboratory result for that day is ever needed, the plant can pull it. That is the kind of accountability a licensed operation is built to provide, and it is the difference between a controlled product and water that merely looks the same.</p>

<h2>The short answer, one more time</h2>
<p>Reverse osmosis water is safe to drink every day when it is re-mineralised afterwards. RO removes what you do not want, re-mineralisation restores what you do. Together they make water that is both clean and good, which is exactly what a proper plant is built to produce.</p>
<p>Want to start daily delivery? Place an <a href="/order">order online</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp, and we will set up a routine that keeps fresh water at your door.</p>',
'Water & Health','reverse osmosis, RO water, re-mineralisation, safe drinking water, minerals','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'Is Reverse Osmosis Water Safe to Drink Every Day? | Pak-Everests',
'RO water is safe to drink daily when it is re-mineralised. Learn what reverse osmosis removes, why minerals are added back, and how to check water safety.',
'is reverse osmosis water safe, RO water safe to drink, mineral water, re-mineralised water, water plant Gujar Khan',
1,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 3 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 3 DAY)),

('how-long-does-a-19-litre-water-bottle-last',
'How Long Does a 19 Litre Water Bottle Last a Home or Office?',
'A simple, realistic guide to how many days a 19 litre bottle lasts for families and offices, so you can plan deliveries without running out or overstocking.',
'<p>A 19 litre water bottle lasts a typical family of four about six to eight days for drinking and cooking, and a small office of six to eight people about three to four days. Those two numbers are the quick answer. The rest of this guide helps you work out your own figure so your <a href="/order">delivery routine</a> matches how much you actually drink.</p>

<h2>The simple maths behind the number</h2>
<p>One 19 litre bottle holds, as the name says, nineteen litres. Health guidance suggests an adult drinks around two to three litres of water a day, more in the Potohar summer. If you also use bottled water for cooking, tea and making baby feeds, real household use is usually higher than pure drinking alone.</p>
<p>Here is a realistic table you can adjust for your own home or workplace.</p>
<ul>
<li>Two adults, drinking only: about 3 to 4 litres a day, so one bottle lasts roughly five to six days.</li>
<li>Family of four, drinking and cooking: about 5 to 7 litres a day, so one bottle lasts roughly three to four days.</li>
<li>Small office of eight people: about 8 to 12 litres a day, so one bottle lasts two to three days.</li>
<li>Busy office of twenty: two to three bottles a day is normal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why summer changes everything</h2>
<p>Water use in Gujar Khan, Rawalpindi and Islamabad is not the same in June as it is in January. In the peak of summer a household can easily drink twice what it drinks in winter, because people sweat more, drink more, and reach for cold water throughout the day. If you plan your deliveries around winter use, you will run out in summer.</p>
<p>The simple fix is to note how many days your first bottle lasts in the current season, then set your delivery a day earlier than that. If a bottle lasts your family four days in summer, book delivery every three days and you will never hit empty.</p>

<h2>How many bottles should you keep at home?</h2>
<p>For most families, two bottles on rotation is the sweet spot. One is on the <a href="/product/water-dispenser">dispenser</a> and in use, the other is the spare. When the spare goes on, you order a refill. This way you always have water, you never store more than you need, and you keep the refundable deposit tied up in only two bottles rather than five.</p>
<p>Offices should keep a slightly larger buffer, because running out during a working day is disruptive. A spare bottle for every dispenser is the minimum, and busy offices often keep one extra beyond that.</p>

<h2>What about the deposit on each bottle?</h2>
<p>Each returnable 19 litre bottle carries a one time refundable security deposit of Rs 1,500. It secures the bottle, not the water, and it comes back to you in full when the bottle is returned in a usable condition. Because the deposit is per bottle, keeping only the bottles you actually need keeps your money working for you rather than sitting in spare containers. We explain the full logic in our post on the <a href="/blog/19-litre-water-bottle-price-and-deposit-explained">19 litre bottle price and deposit</a>.</p>

<h2>Can you work it out with the calculator?</h2>
<p>If your needs are larger, for example a canteen, a construction site or a school, the numbers get big quickly and guessing is risky. Our <a href="/bulk-water-calculator">bulk water calculator</a> lets you enter people and days and returns a clear monthly figure, which is far more reliable than a rough estimate.</p>

<h2>A simple week in a real family home</h2>
<p>Picture a family of four in Gujar Khan through one summer week. On Monday a fresh 19 litre bottle goes on the dispenser and the spare sits ready in the corner. Drinking, tea and cooking draw around six litres a day. By Wednesday evening the bottle on the dispenser is running low, so the spare goes on and a refill is ordered the same night. Thursday morning the free refill arrives and becomes the new spare. The cycle repeats through the week, and the home never once runs dry. The whole system rests on a single habit: order when the spare goes into use, not when the last bottle empties.</p>

<h2>Signs you are ordering too late</h2>
<p>There are clear warning signs that your routine is cutting it too fine. You find yourself rationing water on a hot afternoon. You keep a kettle of boiled water on standby as a backup. You only place an order when the dispenser gurgles empty. Each of these means your buffer is too small for your real use, especially once summer doubles demand. The fix is not to store more bottles and tie up more deposit, it is simply to order one day sooner. Because delivery across our <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a> is free, moving your order forward by a day costs nothing and removes the stress completely.</p>

<h2>How to never run out</h2>
<p>The households that never run out all do the same three things. They know how many days a bottle lasts in the current season, they keep one spare on rotation, and they order the moment the spare goes into use rather than when it runs dry. Free delivery across our <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a> means there is no cost penalty for ordering a day early, so there is no reason to cut it fine.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Does bottled water expire?</h3>
<p>Sealed bottles carry a best before date, and the water is at its best within that window. Once opened and on a dispenser, aim to finish a 19 litre bottle within one to two weeks, which most homes do easily.</p>
<h3>Is it cheaper to buy bigger?</h3>
<p>Per litre, the 19 litre refill is the most economical pack we offer at Rs 250 with free delivery. Smaller packs cost more per litre because of the packaging, but they exist for portability, not for value.</p>
<h3>How many bottles should a new customer start with?</h3>
<p>For a household, start with two bottles, one in use and one spare, plus a dispenser. For an office, start with one spare bottle for every dispenser. Watch how quickly the first bottle empties, then adjust. It is better to begin small and increase once you know your real use than to over order and tie up deposit in bottles you do not need.</p>
<p>Ready to set up a routine that fits your home or office? <a href="/order">Order online</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp and we will match the schedule to your real use.</p>',
'Guides','19 litre bottle, water delivery, home water, office water, planning','Pak-Everests Team',6,
'How Long Does a 19 Litre Water Bottle Last? | Pak-Everests',
'A 19 litre bottle lasts a family about 6 to 8 days and a small office 3 to 4 days. Work out your own figure and plan deliveries so you never run out.',
