('bottled-water-or-boiled-tap-water-rawalpindi-islamabad',
'Bottled Water or Boiled Tap Water in Rawalpindi and Islamabad: Which Is Safer?',
'Boiling kills germs but leaves everything else behind. Here is a clear, honest comparison of boiled tap water and properly bottled mineral water for local homes.',
'<p>Boiling tap water makes it safer to drink by killing germs, but it does not remove dissolved solids, heavy metals or chemical contamination, and it does nothing for taste. Properly bottled mineral water is purified against all of those and is re-mineralised for taste and balance. For day to day drinking in Rawalpindi and Islamabad, that difference matters. This article lays out the honest comparison so you can decide for your own home.</p>

<h2>What boiling does, and what it does not do</h2>
<p>Boiling is genuinely useful. Bringing water to a rolling boil for a minute or two kills bacteria, viruses and parasites, which are the fastest way water makes people ill. If you have no other option, boiled tap water is far better than raw tap water.</p>
<p>But boiling has clear limits, and it is important to be honest about them.</p>
<ul>
<li>It does not remove dissolved solids. Hard water stays hard, and in fact boiling slightly concentrates the minerals as some water turns to steam.</li>
<li>It does not remove heavy metals such as lead or arsenic, which can enter groundwater and old pipes.</li>
<li>It does not remove chemical contamination, nitrates or the taste and smell that come with them.</li>
<li>It uses gas or electricity every single day, which is a running cost that adds up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What proper bottled water does differently</h2>
<p>A <strong>Punjab Food Authority</strong> approved plant like Pak-Everests does not rely on a single step. Water passes through an <a href="/purification-process">eight stage process</a>: sediment filtration, activated carbon, softening, micron filtration, reverse osmosis, re-mineralisation, ultraviolet and ozone. Each stage removes something the previous stage cannot. Boiling addresses germs alone. This process addresses germs, dissolved solids, metals, chemicals, taste and clarity, in that order, on every batch.</p>
<p>The result is water that is not only safe but consistent. It tastes the same in January and July because the minerals are added back under control, which you can read about on our <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">minerals and benefits page</a>.</p>

<h2>Is tap water in Rawalpindi and Islamabad bad?</h2>
<p>The honest answer is that it varies a great deal by area, by building and by season. Some neighbourhoods have reasonable supply, others struggle with old pipes, storage tanks that are rarely cleaned, and pressure problems that let contamination in. The problem is that you cannot see, smell or taste most of what matters, so you cannot judge safety from the tap alone. This uncertainty is the real reason many families move to bottled water. It removes the guesswork.</p>

<h2>Comparing the true cost</h2>
<p>People often assume boiling is free because the water is already there. It is not. Boiling several litres a day uses gas or electricity, ties up a pot and a stove, and takes time, every day, forever. Bottled water has a clear, predictable cost instead. A <a href="/product/19-litre-refill-bottle">19 litre refill</a> is Rs 250 with free delivery across our <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a>, and for most families that works out to a very small daily amount for water you never have to think about again.</p>

<h2>When is boiling the right choice?</h2>
<p>Boiling still has its place. If bottled water is genuinely unavailable, boiling is a sensible emergency step. It is also fine for uses where taste and minerals do not matter, such as some cooking. But as a permanent, daily drinking solution for a family, boiled tap water leaves too much behind to be the best answer.</p>

<h2>What about filter jugs and home RO units?</h2>
<p>Home filters sit between boiling and bottled water, and they are worth understanding honestly. A simple filter jug improves taste and removes some particles, but it does little against dissolved metals or germs, and the cartridge must be changed on time or it slowly becomes part of the problem itself. A home reverse osmosis unit does far more, and a good one with a re-mineralisation stage can produce genuinely good water. But home units need regular membrane and filter changes, waste some water during filtration, and depend entirely on the owner maintaining them correctly. Skip the servicing and the quality quietly drops with no warning sign on the glass.</p>
<p>Bottled water from a licensed plant removes that maintenance burden from you altogether. The process, the testing and the batch records are the plants responsibility, not yours, and every bottle is traceable. For a family that wants reliable water without becoming its own water technician, that is the real appeal.</p>

<h2>The convenience factor, especially with children</h2>
<p>Convenience is not a small thing when a household is busy. Boiling means planning ahead, letting water cool, and finding fridge space for it, every single day. With young children who drink often and unpredictably, that becomes a constant chore. A <a href="/product/water-dispenser">dispenser</a> fed by bottled water gives cool water instantly, at any hour, with no preparation at all. Many families move to bottled water not only for safety but because it removes one recurring task from an already full day, which is a benefit you feel every morning.</p>

<h2>The balanced conclusion</h2>
<p>Boiled tap water is safer than raw tap water, but it treats only one of several problems. Properly bottled mineral water treats all of them and adds consistent taste and balance on top. For everyday drinking by families in Rawalpindi, Islamabad and across the Potohar region, bottled water from a licensed plant is the more complete and more convenient choice.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can I mix both?</h3>
<p>Many homes do. They keep bottled water for drinking and use boiled or filtered tap water for some cooking. There is nothing wrong with a sensible mix.</p>
<h3>Is boiling enough during a supply problem?</h3>
<p>As a short term emergency measure, yes. If bottled water is genuinely unavailable during a disruption, a rolling boil for a minute or two makes tap water safer against germs and is far better than drinking it raw. Just remember what boiling does not fix: dissolved solids, metals and chemical contamination all remain. Treat boiling as a stopgap that buys you time, not as a permanent substitute for properly purified water. As soon as normal supply returns, move drinking and cooking back to a licensed bottled source.</p>
<h3>How do I start bottled delivery?</h3>
<p>Place an <a href="/order">order online</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp. We deliver free across Gujar Khan, Rawalpindi, Islamabad and the surrounding towns, and we will set a routine that fits your household.</p>',
'Water & Health','bottled water vs boiled water, tap water Rawalpindi, safe drinking water Islamabad, mineral water','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'Bottled Water or Boiled Tap Water: Which Is Safer? | Pak-Everests',
'Boiling kills germs but leaves dissolved solids and metals behind. Compare boiled tap water and bottled mineral water for homes in Rawalpindi and Islamabad.',
'bottled water vs boiled water, is tap water safe in Islamabad, safe drinking water Rawalpindi, mineral water plant',
0,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 7 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)),

('how-to-store-drinking-water-bottles-in-summer',
'How to Store Drinking Water Bottles Correctly in Summer',
'Where and how you store your water bottles changes how it tastes and how long it stays fresh. Nine simple storage rules for the Potohar summer.',
'<p>Store drinking water bottles in a cool, shaded, clean place away from direct sunlight, strong smells and chemicals, and keep them off the bare floor. Do that and your water stays fresh and tastes the way it should. Ignore it, especially in the Potohar summer, and even the best water can pick up an off taste. Here are the storage rules that actually matter.</p>

<h2>Why storage matters more in summer</h2>
<p>Heat speeds everything up. A bottle left in the sun on a hot Gujar Khan afternoon can warm to an unpleasant temperature, and warm water is the ideal environment for anything unwanted to develop if a seal is ever compromised. Sunlight and heat are the two things to protect your water from, and both are in plentiful supply here from May to September.</p>

<h2>The nine storage rules</h2>
<h3>1. Keep bottles out of direct sunlight</h3>
<p>Sunlight is the single biggest enemy of stored water. Ultraviolet light and heat together can encourage growth and can slowly affect taste. Store bottles indoors or in deep shade, never on a sunny balcony or in a parked vehicle.</p>
<h3>2. Choose the coolest spot you have</h3>
<p>A cool store room, a shaded utility area or the lower shelf of a pantry all work well. The cooler and more stable the temperature, the longer the water stays at its best.</p>
<h3>3. Keep bottles off the bare floor</h3>
<p>Stand bottles on a rack, a pallet or a clean mat rather than directly on concrete or soil. This keeps the base clean and stops damp and dust building up around the cap area.</p>
<h3>4. Store away from strong smells and chemicals</h3>
<p>Plastic can, over time, pick up strong odours from its surroundings. Never store drinking water next to petrol, paint, cleaning chemicals, onions or anything with a powerful smell. This is doubly important for the returnable <a href="/product/19-litre-refill-bottle">19 litre bottles</a>, which is exactly why we ask that they are never used for anything other than water.</p>
<h3>5. Keep the cap and neck clean</h3>
<p>Most contamination enters at the cap and neck, not through the walls of the bottle. Wipe the top of a bottle before placing it on the <a href="/product/water-dispenser">dispenser</a>, and keep the dispenser tap clean.</p>
<h3>6. Use older stock first</h3>
<p>Rotate your bottles so the oldest is used next. This simple habit means water is always drunk well within its best before window.</p>
<h3>7. Do not overstock</h3>
<p>Because we deliver free across the <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a>, there is no need to hoard bottles. Keeping just a spare or two on rotation means everything you drink is fresh, and your refundable deposit is not tied up in bottles sitting in a corner.</p>
<h3>8. Handle small packs the same way</h3>
<p>The same rules apply to <a href="/product/500ml-pack-of-12-pure">500 ml</a> and 1.5 litre packs. Keep the shrink wrapped packs shaded and cool until you open them, then treat the loose bottles as you would any drinking water.</p>
<h3>9. Clean the dispenser regularly</h3>
<p>A bottle is only as clean as what it sits on. Rinse and wipe the dispenser, including the spike and the tap, every time you change a bottle, and give it a fuller clean every few weeks.</p>

<h2>What surface should bottles stand on?</h2>
<p>The best surface is a clean, raised, stable one. A simple wooden or plastic rack, a pallet, or even a clean tray keeps the base of the bottle off bare concrete and away from ground damp and dust. Avoid standing bottles directly on soil, on a bathroom floor, or anywhere they can be splashed with waste water. For the returnable 19 litre bottles this matters most around the cap and neck, since that is where contamination enters. A raised surface also makes it easier to wipe underneath and keep the whole area clean.</p>

<h2>Storing water in a car or outdoors</h2>
<p>A parked car in a Gujar Khan summer is the worst place you can leave drinking water. The interior can reach extreme temperatures within minutes, heating the water and, if a seal is weak, encouraging anything unwanted to develop. If you must carry water in a vehicle, keep it in the shaded footwell rather than on a sunny seat or in the boot, and do not leave it there for days. Outdoors, the same rule applies twice over: never store bottles on an open roof, balcony or yard where the sun reaches them. Shade is not a nice extra in this climate, it is the whole point.</p>

<h2>A quick weekly cleaning routine</h2>
<p>A five minute weekly habit keeps everything hygienic. Wipe the top and neck of each stored bottle, clean the dispenser tap and the spike where the bottle sits, and wipe down the rack or surface. Every few weeks, give the dispenser a fuller clean following its instructions. None of this is difficult, but done regularly it protects the water far more than most people expect, because the container and the tap are where problems usually begin.</p>

<h2>How long does stored water stay fresh?</h2>
<p>Unopened and stored correctly, sealed bottles stay fresh right up to their best before date. Once a 19 litre bottle is opened and on a dispenser, aim to finish it within one to two weeks. In practice most homes and offices use it far faster than that, especially in summer.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is it safe to keep water in the fridge for a long time?</h3>
<p>Chilled water in the fridge is fine and often tastes better, but keep it covered so it does not absorb fridge odours, and finish an opened bottle within a week or so. The fridge slows things down, it does not stop them, so rotation still matters.</p>
<h3>Can I refill small bottles from a 19 litre bottle?</h3>
<p>You can, for convenience, as long as the small bottle is clean and you drink the water soon. Just do not let refilled bottles sit around warm for days, and never reuse a bottle that has held anything other than water. A clean bottle and prompt use are the whole trick.</p>

<h2>The one habit that matters most</h2>
<p>If you remember only one thing, remember shade. Keeping bottles out of the sun and heat protects taste and freshness more than any other single step. Everything else on this list is good practice, but shade is the rule that summer punishes hardest when you break it.</p>
<p>Need a fresh delivery or a spare bottle before the next hot week? <a href="/order">Order online</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp, and we will keep you stocked.</p>',
'Guides','water storage, summer, bottle care, dispenser cleaning, freshness','Pak-Everests Team',6,
'How to Store Drinking Water Bottles Correctly in Summer | Pak-Everests',
'Keep water bottles cool, shaded, clean and off the floor to protect taste and freshness. Nine simple storage rules for the Potohar summer.',
'how to store water bottles, water storage summer, keep water fresh, water dispenser cleaning, bottled water Gujar Khan',
0,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 9 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 9 DAY)),

('what-makes-water-taste-good-balanced-minerals',
'What Makes Water Taste Good? The Simple Science of Balanced Minerals',
'Why does one glass of water taste fresh and another taste flat or metallic? The answer is minerals, and getting the balance right is a science, not an accident.',
'<p>Water tastes good when it carries a balanced amount of natural minerals, mainly calcium, magnesium, potassium and a little sodium. Too few minerals and water tastes flat and empty. Too many, or the wrong ones, and it tastes salty, metallic or bitter. Great tasting water sits in the balanced middle, and reaching that balance on every batch is a science that a good plant controls on purpose.</p>

<h2>Why does pure water taste flat?</h2>
<p>It surprises people, but completely pure water does not taste refreshing. It tastes of almost nothing, a little flat, even slightly dull. That is because the fresh, clean taste we associate with good water actually comes from the minerals dissolved in it. Remove every mineral, as reverse osmosis does, and you remove the taste along with the impurities. This is why water straight off an RO membrane, before anything is added back, is clean but lifeless.</p>

<h2>The four minerals that shape taste</h2>
<p>Each of the main minerals plays a role, and you can read more about their health side on our <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">minerals and benefits page</a>. In taste terms, here is what they do.</p>
<h3>Calcium</h3>
<p>Calcium gives water a clean, slightly sweet, rounded character. In balance it is the backbone of a pleasant taste. In excess it makes water taste chalky and hard.</p>
<h3>Magnesium</h3>
<p>Magnesium adds a subtle crispness and freshness. A small amount lifts the taste. Too much turns bitter, which is why it has to be controlled carefully.</p>
<h3>Potassium</h3>
<p>Potassium is important for the body and, in small amounts, keeps the mineral profile balanced without dominating the taste.</p>
<h3>Sodium</h3>
<p>A very small amount of sodium rounds out the flavour. But sodium is the one to keep low, because even a little too much makes water taste salty, and high sodium is not what you want in daily drinking water.</p>

<h2>Why balance is harder than it sounds</h2>
<p>Getting minerals back into water is easy. Getting them in the right ratio, at the right total level, on every single batch, is the hard part. Raw groundwater around Gujar Khan and the wider Potohar region is not the same in every well or every season, so you cannot simply bottle it and hope. A controlled plant strips the water back to a clean base with reverse osmosis, then adds a measured mineral blend back in. Because it starts from the same clean base each time, the finished taste is consistent all year.</p>
<p>This is the real reason Pak-Everests runs re-mineralisation as a deliberate stage in its <a href="/purification-process">eight stage process</a> rather than leaving taste to chance.</p>

<h2>What is TDS and does a higher number mean better taste?</h2>
<p>TDS stands for total dissolved solids, a measure of everything dissolved in the water. It is often misunderstood. A very low TDS means flat, empty water. A very high TDS usually means hard or salty water. Good tasting drinking water sits in a comfortable middle band, where there are enough of the right minerals to taste fresh but not so many that it turns hard or salty. TDS alone does not tell you if water is good, because it does not say which solids are present. Balance matters more than the raw number, a point we explore in our post on <a href="/blog/what-is-tds-in-drinking-water-good-level">TDS and what a good level is</a>.</p>

<h2>Why temperature also changes taste</h2>
<p>Even perfectly balanced water tastes different warm and cool. Cool water tastes cleaner and more refreshing because cold slightly mutes any strong notes and lifts the fresh ones. This is one reason a <a href="/product/water-dispenser">hot and cold dispenser</a> is so popular in homes and offices, it lets you serve the water at the temperature where it tastes its best.</p>

<h2>Does the bottle or container affect taste?</h2>
<p>It can, which is another reason storage matters. Water is good at picking up flavours from its surroundings, so a bottle stored next to strong smelling items, or a container previously used for anything other than water, can taint even perfectly balanced water. This is exactly why the returnable 19 litre bottles must only ever hold water, and why we inspect and wash every returned bottle before it is refilled. Fresh, clean packaging is part of good taste, not just good hygiene. If your water suddenly tastes different from a new bottle, the container or its storage is the first thing to check.</p>

<h2>How to run a fair taste comparison at home</h2>
<p>If you want to judge two waters honestly, do it properly. Pour each into an identical clean glass, let both come to the same room temperature, and taste them one after another with a plain cracker in between to reset your mouth. Note which tastes flat and empty, which tastes hard, salty or metallic, and which tastes clean and rounded with nothing lingering. Doing this at matched temperature is important, because cold hides differences and can make even poor water seem fine. A fair comparison at room temperature is where balanced <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">mineral water</a> shows its quality most clearly.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Why does bottled water taste better than my tap water?</h3>
<p>Usually because it is both purified and balanced. Tap water can carry hardness, chlorine or a metallic edge from pipes, all of which affect taste. Properly bottled water has those removed by the process and then has a measured, balanced set of minerals added back, so it tastes clean and fresh rather than hard or flat.</p>
<h3>Does adding more minerals always improve taste?</h3>
<p>No. Beyond a certain point more minerals make water taste hard, chalky or salty. The goal is balance, not maximum mineral content. A moderate, well judged blend tastes far better than an overloaded one, which is why re-mineralisation is dosed carefully rather than piled on.</p>

<h2>The taste test that tells you a lot</h2>
<p>Here is a simple test. Pour a glass of your current water, let it come to room temperature, and taste it slowly. Flat and empty suggests over filtered water with no minerals added back. Salty or metallic suggests poor balance or contamination. Clean, fresh and slightly rounded, with nothing that lingers unpleasantly, is the sign of properly balanced mineral water. That last one is what a good plant is built to deliver in every bottle.</p>
<p>Want to taste the difference for yourself? <a href="/order">Order a bottle</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp, and judge it by the glass.</p>',
'Water & Health','water taste, minerals, calcium, magnesium, TDS, re-mineralisation','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'What Makes Water Taste Good? The Science of Minerals | Pak-Everests',
'Water tastes good when calcium, magnesium, potassium and sodium are in balance. Learn why pure water is flat and how a plant controls taste on every batch.',
'what makes water taste good, mineral water taste, TDS in water, balanced minerals, mineral water Gujar Khan',
0,'published',DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 12 DAY),DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 12 DAY)),

('office-water-supply-guide-dha-islamabad-bahria-town',
'A Complete Office Water Supply Guide for DHA Islamabad and Bahria Town',
'Everything an office manager needs to set up reliable drinking water: how much to order, dispensers, billing, and how to make sure you never run dry mid-week.',
'<p>Setting up drinking water for an office is simple once you get three things right: the right number of bottles for your headcount, a reliable dispenser on each floor, and a delivery routine that refills before you run out. This guide walks office managers in DHA Islamabad, Bahria Town Rawalpindi and the surrounding areas through the whole setup, start to finish.</p>

<h2>Step one: work out how much water your office needs</h2>
<p>A working adult drinks more water at the office than most people expect, especially in summer, because tea, coffee and cold water all draw from the same supply. As a planning rule, allow one to one and a half litres per person per working day.</p>
<ul>
<li>A team of 8 uses roughly 10 to 12 litres a day, about three <a href="/product/19-litre-refill-bottle">19 litre bottles</a> a week.</li>
<li>A team of 20 uses roughly 25 to 30 litres a day, around eight to ten bottles a week.</li>
<li>A team of 50 uses well over 60 litres a day, and needs a planned weekly schedule.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want an exact figure, our <a href="/bulk-water-calculator">bulk water calculator</a> turns headcount and days into a clear monthly total.</p>

<h2>Step two: choose the right dispensers</h2>
<p>One dispenser per floor is the minimum, and busy floors benefit from one near the kitchen and one near the work area to avoid queues. A hot and cold <a href="/product/water-dispenser">dispenser</a> covers tea, coffee and chilled water from a single unit, which is what most offices want. You can buy a dispenser outright for Rs 42,000 or take it on rent for Rs 2,000 per month with servicing included, which keeps the setup cost low when you are opening a new office.</p>

<h2>Step three: set a delivery routine, not one off orders</h2>
<p>The mistake that causes empty dispensers on a Wednesday afternoon is ordering only when you notice you are low. Offices run best on a fixed routine. Decide how many bottles you use in a week, keep a spare for every dispenser, and set a standing delivery so refills arrive before the spares run out. We deliver free across our full <a href="/coverage-areas">coverage area</a>, so ordering ahead of time costs nothing.</p>

<h2>Step four: keep the paperwork clean</h2>
<p>Offices need records, not guesswork. Every Pak-Everests order is logged, and we can provide clear billing for your accounts team. If your office prefers a formal arrangement, we can put a simple supply agreement in place that sets out volumes, pricing and delivery days, which makes budgeting and audits straightforward.</p>

<h2>Custom labelled water for client facing offices</h2>
<p>If your office hosts clients, meetings or events, branded water leaves a strong impression. Our <a href="/custom-label-bottles">custom label bottles</a> put your company name and logo on the <a href="/product/500ml-pack-of-12-pure">500 ml packs</a> used in meeting rooms and receptions. It is an inexpensive touch that makes a boardroom look considered and professional.</p>

<h2>Why local supply reliability matters</h2>
<p>DHA Islamabad, Bahria Town and the wider Rawalpindi and Islamabad market are exactly the areas we are built to serve. Reliability in this business is measured in summer, when demand doubles, and a supplier who is close and well organised keeps arriving when others fall behind. Being based in the Potohar region and running our own delivery means your office is not at the end of a long, fragile supply chain.</p>

<h2>Planning for summer peaks and events</h2>
<p>Office water use is not flat across the year. In the peak of a Rawalpindi or Islamabad summer, consumption can rise sharply as staff drink more and reach for chilled water all day. A routine built around winter use will leave dispensers empty by mid afternoon in June. The simple answer is to review your standing order at the start of summer and lift it, then bring it back down in the cooler months. Events and busy periods need the same foresight. If you are hosting a training day, a client visit or a large meeting, add extra bottles and, where it suits, branded <a href="/product/500ml-pack-of-12-pure">500 ml packs</a> for the room, ordered a few days ahead rather than on the morning itself.</p>

<h2>Hygiene standards your team will notice</h2>
<p>Staff notice water quality more than managers expect, because they drink it all day. Clean dispensers, sealed bottles with visible batch codes, and water that tastes consistently fresh all signal that the office is well run. Supplying water from a <strong>Punjab Food Authority</strong> approved plant, purified through a full <a href="/purification-process">eight stage process</a>, is a small decision that quietly supports staff wellbeing and reflects well on the workplace. It is the kind of detail that a professional office gets right without being asked.</p>

<h2>Switching supplier without disruption</h2>
<p>If you are already with another supplier and considering a change, it is simpler than it sounds. Return their bottles and recover any deposit, take on your new bottles and dispensers, and set the standing delivery from day one. A short overlap, keeping a few of the old bottles until the first new delivery lands, means there is no gap in supply at any point. We handle this changeover regularly for offices across the area and can plan it around your working week.</p>

<h2>A simple setup checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Count your team and estimate one to one and a half litres per person per day.</li>
<li>Place one dispenser per floor, more on busy floors.</li>
<li>Keep one spare bottle per dispenser at all times.</li>
<li>Set a standing weekly delivery, adjusted up for summer.</li>
<li>Order branded 500 ml packs for meeting rooms if you host clients.</li>
<li>Ask for logged billing or a supply agreement for your accounts team.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can you deliver on fixed days?</h3>
<p>Yes. Most offices prefer fixed delivery days, and we set the schedule around your working week.</p>
<h3>Do you service dispensers?</h3>
<p>Rented dispensers include servicing. For purchased units we can advise on cleaning and arrange support.</p>
<h3>Can you provide branded water for a company event?</h3>
<p>Yes. For conferences, launches and client days we can supply custom labelled 500 ml packs carrying your company name and logo, ordered a few days ahead so there is time to print and pack. It is an inexpensive touch that makes an event look considered, and staff often keep using the branded bottles around the office afterwards. Ask us early and we will plan the quantity around your guest numbers.</p>
<p>Ready to set up your office? <a href="/contact">Contact us</a>, place an <a href="/order">order online</a>, or message 0332 2901309 on WhatsApp and we will build a routine around your team.</p>',
'Guides','office water supply, dispenser, DHA Islamabad, Bahria Town, business water','Pak-Everests Team',7,
