('what-is-tds-in-drinking-water-good-level',
'What Is TDS in Drinking Water and What Is a Good Level?',
'TDS is one of the most quoted and most misunderstood numbers in drinking water. Here is what it really measures, what a good level looks like, and what it does not tell you.',
'<p>TDS stands for total dissolved solids, and it measures the combined amount of everything dissolved in water, mostly minerals and salts, in milligrams per litre. For good drinking water, a moderate TDS in a comfortable middle band is ideal. Very low TDS means flat, empty water, and very high TDS usually means hard or salty water. But TDS alone does not tell you whether water is safe, and this article explains why balance matters more than the number.</p>

<h2>What does TDS actually measure?</h2>
<p>Every source of water carries dissolved substances picked up as it moves through soil and rock. These include the good minerals, calcium, magnesium, potassium and sodium, and also less desirable dissolved salts and, in some places, contaminants. A TDS meter adds all of this up into one figure. That is its strength and its weakness. It gives you a single quick number, but it cannot tell you which solids make up that number.</p>

<h2>What is a good TDS level for drinking water?</h2>
<p>There is no single magic figure, but there is a sensible range. Water with a very low TDS, close to zero, is the kind that comes straight off a reverse osmosis membrane before minerals are added back. It is clean but tastes flat and gives you nothing. Water with a very high TDS tends to taste hard, chalky or salty and can be unpleasant to drink daily. Good drinking water sits comfortably in between, where there are enough of the right minerals for a fresh, rounded taste without tipping into hardness. The exact figure matters less than what those solids are and whether they are balanced.</p>

<h2>Why a low TDS is not automatically better</h2>
<p>Some filters are sold on the promise of driving TDS as low as possible, as if lower always means healthier. It does not. As we explain in our article on <a href="/blog/what-makes-water-taste-good-balanced-minerals">what makes water taste good</a>, the minerals that give water its fresh taste are dissolved solids too. Strip them all out and you get water that is technically very pure but flat and empty, contributing nothing. This is exactly why Pak-Everests re-mineralises after reverse osmosis, deliberately restoring a balanced level of the right minerals rather than chasing the lowest possible number.</p>

<h2>Why a high TDS is a warning worth checking</h2>
<p>A high TDS reading is a reason to look closer, not to panic. It often simply means hard water, heavy in calcium and magnesium salts, which is common in groundwater across the Potohar region. That is more a taste and scale nuisance than a danger. But a high figure can also include dissolved contaminants you do not want, and the meter cannot tell the difference. High TDS is a prompt to find out what is in the water, which is where a full purification process and testing matter.</p>

<h2>The big limitation: TDS says nothing about germs</h2>
<p>This is the most important point and the one most often missed. A TDS meter measures dissolved solids only. It does not detect bacteria, viruses or many chemical contaminants. Water can show a perfectly moderate TDS and still be unsafe to drink because of germs the meter cannot see. This is why TDS is a useful indicator of taste and mineral load, but never a certificate of safety. Safety comes from a controlled process, disinfection steps such as ultraviolet and ozone, and traceable batch records, all of which our <a href="/purification-process">eight stage process</a> provides.</p>

<h2>How Pak-Everests uses TDS the right way</h2>
<p>In a properly run plant, TDS is a control tool, not a marketing slogan. Reverse osmosis first brings the dissolved solids right down to a clean, consistent base, removing the unwanted salts and contaminants. Then re-mineralisation lifts the TDS back into the balanced band on purpose, adding measured amounts of calcium, magnesium, potassium and a little sodium. The result is water with a sensible, consistent TDS made up of the minerals you actually want, batch after batch. You can read the health side of those minerals on our <a href="/minerals-and-benefits">minerals and benefits page</a>.</p>

<h2>How to read a TDS claim on a label or advert</h2>
<p>Once you understand TDS, you can read water marketing more critically. A brand that boasts of an extremely low TDS is really telling you the water is heavily filtered and probably not re-mineralised, which means flat, empty water. A brand that quotes a very high TDS as proof of being mineral rich may simply be selling hard water. The honest position is the one in the middle: a balanced, moderate level made up of the right minerals, added back on purpose. When you see a TDS number, ask what the solids are, not just how big the figure is. The number without that context tells you very little.</p>

<h2>TDS, scale and household appliances</h2>
<p>TDS also shows up around the home in a practical way. Hard water with a high mineral load leaves the white, chalky scale you see building up inside a kettle, on taps and on the heating element of an iron or geyser. This scale is a nuisance, shortening the life of appliances and forcing more frequent cleaning. Water that has been brought to a balanced level through reverse osmosis and controlled re-mineralisation is far gentler on kettles and appliances than raw hard groundwater, which is a small everyday benefit of drinking properly treated water rather than untreated supply.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Should I buy a TDS meter?</h3>
<p>A TDS meter is a fun way to see mineral load, but do not treat its number as proof of safety. It cannot detect germs or many chemicals. Buying from a licensed plant tells you far more than a meter reading ever will.</p>
<h3>Is higher mineral water better for health?</h3>
<p>Balanced is better than high or low. A moderate, balanced mineral level gives you fresh taste and a small, useful contribution, without the downsides of very hard or very salty water.</p>
<h3>What TDS does Pak-Everests water have?</h3>
<p>Our water is re-mineralised to a balanced, consistent level after reverse osmosis, aimed at fresh taste and quality rather than an extreme number. To taste it for yourself, <a href="/order">order a bottle</a> or message 0333 5592206 on WhatsApp.</p>',
'Water & Health','TDS, total dissolved solids, water quality, mineral water, RO water','Pak-Everests Team',7,
'What Is TDS in Drinking Water and What Is a Good Level? | Pak-Everests',
